1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,483
Several devices share a medium, but none can
instantly know what

2
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:10,967
every other device is doing. When may one
transmit? This local

3
00:00:10,967 --> 00:00:16,450
coordination problem lies behind access
protocols, and the answer begins with

4
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:21,933
a collision.

5
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:27,742
A and C start before either hears the other.
Their signals

6
00:00:27,742 --> 00:00:33,550
overlap, making these transmissions fail.
Watch the red disturbance spread. The

7
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:39,358
useful question is how each station learns
enough to decide what

8
00:00:39,358 --> 00:00:45,167
happens next.

9
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:50,867
Begin with almost no coordination. Pure ALOHA
lets a station send

10
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:56,567
whenever data is ready. Simplicity comes at a
cost: another transmission

11
00:00:56,567 --> 00:01:02,267
can overlap at many different starting times,
forcing failed attempts to

12
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:07,967
be repeated.

13
00:01:07,967 --> 00:01:13,558
Let a frame last T. Another frame can
interfere if it

14
00:01:13,558 --> 00:01:19,150
starts within T before our start, or T
afterward. Slide those

15
00:01:19,150 --> 00:01:24,742
possibilities together: the vulnerable
interval spans two T, twice the frame's

16
00:01:24,742 --> 00:01:30,333
duration.

17
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:35,925
If colliding stations wait exactly the same
time, they can collide

18
00:01:35,925 --> 00:01:41,517
again on the retry. Random waits break that
symmetry. They're no

19
00:01:41,517 --> 00:01:47,108
guarantee for an individual attempt, but
reduce the chance of repeating

20
00:01:47,108 --> 00:01:52,700
the same overlap.

21
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:58,400
Now mark shared starting boundaries. Slotted
ALOHA permits transmission only at

22
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:04,100
those boundaries, with frames fitting the
slots. Stations can still choose

23
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:09,800
the same slot, but arbitrary partial overlaps
no longer create additional

24
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:15,500
collision opportunities.

25
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:23,100
Watch the vulnerable interval shrink. Only
another attempt in the same

26
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:30,700
slot conflicts with this frame. Coordinating
the starts reduces the interval

27
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:38,300
from two T to T, without changing the physical
transmission rate.

28
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:43,783
In the ideal model, pure ALOHA peaks near
eighteen point four

29
00:02:43,783 --> 00:02:49,267
percent throughput; slotted ALOHA near thirty-
six point eight. Push attempts past

30
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:54,750
the peak and collisions dominate. More
activity can actually produce less

31
00:02:54,750 --> 00:03:00,233
useful delivery.

32
00:03:00,233 --> 00:03:05,933
There are two broad ways to organize access:
assign turns, or

33
00:03:05,933 --> 00:03:11,633
let stations compete under shared rules. A
schedule needs coordination; contention

34
00:03:11,633 --> 00:03:17,333
needs a way to handle conflicting attempts.
Both must divide finite

35
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:23,033
capacity.

36
00:03:23,033 --> 00:03:28,733
What if we listen first? Carrier sensing lets
a station check

37
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:34,433
whether the medium appears busy before
transmitting. That information avoids some

38
00:03:34,433 --> 00:03:40,133
blind collisions, but a quiet local
observation isn't perfect knowledge of

39
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:45,833
the whole network.

40
00:03:45,833 --> 00:03:51,750
Here is the remaining problem: signals take
time to travel. A

41
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:57,667
distant station can start while the first
signal is still approaching

42
00:03:57,667 --> 00:04:03,583
it. Both hear silence, both transmit, and
neither has broken the

43
00:04:03,583 --> 00:04:09,500
listening rule.

44
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:15,200
One-persistent CSMA listens continuously and
transmits as soon as the channel

45
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:20,900
becomes idle. That eagerness limits waiting,
but several waiting stations can

46
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:26,600
react to the same silence together, producing
another collision at the

47
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:32,300
restart.

48
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:37,892
Nonpersistent CSMA waits a random interval
after finding the channel busy,

49
00:04:37,892 --> 00:04:43,483
then checks again. Restart attempts become
less synchronized. The trade-off is

50
00:04:43,483 --> 00:04:49,075
visible in the empty gap: everyone might still
be waiting after

51
00:04:49,075 --> 00:04:54,667
the medium becomes free.

52
00:04:54,667 --> 00:05:00,258
In a suitable slotted setting, p-persistent
CSMA makes sending probabilistic. On

53
00:05:00,258 --> 00:05:05,850
an idle slot, transmit with probability p or
defer. Adjusting that

54
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:11,442
chance spreads competing attempts, balancing
prompt use against the risk of

55
00:05:11,442 --> 00:05:17,033
simultaneous starts.

56
00:05:17,033 --> 00:05:22,517
For the next mechanism, keep one historical
setting in mind: shared,

57
00:05:22,517 --> 00:05:28,000
half-duplex Ethernet. CSMA/CD senses before
sending and detects collisions during
transmission.

58
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,483
These rules describe that shared medium,
rather than every Ethernet or

59
00:05:33,483 --> 00:05:38,967
wireless connection.

60
00:05:38,967 --> 00:05:44,875
After detecting a collision, stop the frame,
signal the collision with

61
00:05:44,875 --> 00:05:50,783
a jam, and back off. An unsuccessful
transmission ends before wasting

62
00:05:50,783 --> 00:05:56,692
a whole frame. The stations then use different
retry delays to

63
00:05:56,692 --> 00:06:02,600
separate their next attempts.

64
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:08,408
A far-end collision needs time to become
visible at the sender.

65
00:06:08,408 --> 00:06:14,217
The sender must still be transmitting when
that indication returns. That's

66
00:06:14,217 --> 00:06:20,025
why minimum frame duration and the network's
round-trip propagation delay are

67
00:06:20,025 --> 00:06:25,833
connected.

68
00:06:25,833 --> 00:06:31,533
After repeated collisions, widen the random
range: two choices, then four,

69
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:37,233
then eight. Binary exponential backoff gives
competing attempts more room to

70
00:06:37,233 --> 00:06:42,933
spread. It changes the distribution of waits,
rather than guaranteeing a

71
00:06:42,933 --> 00:06:48,633
dedicated turn.

72
00:06:48,633 --> 00:06:54,442
After the first collision, choose zero or one
slot; after the

73
00:06:54,442 --> 00:07:00,250
second, zero through three. Follow the two
choices in the picture.

74
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:06,058
Different selections create different retry
times, reducing the chance of another

75
00:07:06,058 --> 00:07:11,867
synchronized failure.

76
00:07:11,867 --> 00:07:17,675
A completed backoff doesn't reserve the
medium. Another station may already

77
00:07:17,675 --> 00:07:23,483
be transmitting when the wait ends. The sender
still follows the

78
00:07:23,483 --> 00:07:29,292
channel rules. A timer tells it when to try,
not that

79
00:07:29,292 --> 00:07:35,100
success is guaranteed.

80
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:40,908
Now replace the shared wire with switched
full-duplex links. Separate sending

81
00:07:40,908 --> 00:07:46,717
and receiving paths allow opposite flows
together. The old shared-medium collision

82
00:07:46,717 --> 00:07:52,525
mechanism no longer applies on these links,
and CSMA/CD isn't used

83
00:07:52,525 --> 00:07:58,333
there.

84
00:07:58,333 --> 00:08:03,925
Wi-Fi faces different observation limits. A
station doesn't simply detect collisions

85
00:08:03,925 --> 00:08:09,517
during transmission in Ethernet's way.
Contention, avoidance rules, and
acknowledgments help

86
00:08:09,517 --> 00:08:15,108
coordinate access when different stations may
hear different parts of the

87
00:08:15,108 --> 00:08:20,700
wireless network.

88
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:26,292
Compare what each station knows. ALOHA sends;
CSMA first senses; CSMA/CD

89
00:08:26,292 --> 00:08:31,883
also detects collisions in its shared-wire
setting. Adding information changes the

90
00:08:31,883 --> 00:08:37,475
rules, while propagation delay still limits
how quickly that information can

91
00:08:37,475 --> 00:08:43,067
arrive.

92
00:08:43,067 --> 00:08:48,550
Return to the collision. Ask what each sender
could hear, when

93
00:08:48,550 --> 00:08:54,033
it could start, and how it chooses a retry.
Those three

94
00:08:54,033 --> 00:08:59,517
questions turn a list of protocol names into
understandable responses to

95
00:08:59,517 --> 00:09:05,000
one shared-medium problem.
